WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.140
Holy refreshing sense of humility, Batman!

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.100
It sounds like Christian Bale really did know
when to say goodbye to his role as the Dark

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:07.600
Knight.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.640
Bale has been hot on the publicity trail of
late in support of his new race car drama

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.700
Ford v Ferrari, in which he plays a racer
opposite Matt Damon's mild-mannered car designer.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.560
Recently, the Ford v Ferrari press campaign
found the former Batman actor sitting down

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.920
with the folks at The Toronto Sun, who used
part of their time with him to ask a question

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.510
that's long been on the minds of fans devoted
to Bale's iconic work in Christopher Nolan's

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.820
Dark Knight trilogy.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.880
The question, of course, was this: "Why didn't
Bale sign on for a Dark Knight 4?"

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.360
Bale's answer to the question is as simple
as it is surprisingly humble.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.130
The actor claimed he ultimately decided a
fourth turn as the Caped Crusader would have

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.520
been, quote, "overindulgent."

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.640
That's right — in a time where stars and
studios seem to keep lining up blockbuster

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.680
sequels to milk every single penny out of
a franchise, Bale, long regarded as a true

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.699
"actor's actor," apparently told Warner Bros.
"thanks but no thanks" when the studio approached

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.820
him about reprising his role as Bruce Wayne
for another Dark Knight venture.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.580
And if Bale is to be believed, he did so largely
because he didn't want to overstay his welcome

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.260
in the role.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.840
"More copycats last night, Alfred, with guns."

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.500
"Why don't you hire them and take the weekend
off?"

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.520
"That wasn't exactly what I meant when I said
I wanted to inspire people."

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.799
It's worth nothing that Bale's decision was
also greatly influenced by the fact that Nolan

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.200
was indeed moving on from the Dark Knight
franchise.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.440
As Bale continued to wax poetic about why
he didn't return for a fourth Dark Knight

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.960
flick, he admitted as much — noting that,
outside of his own wishes to leave Batman

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.720
behind, his decision to walk away from what
would've no doubt been a mammoth payday for

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.440
Dark Knight 4 was equally out of respect for
Christopher Nolan himself.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:44.520
Bale explained:

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.079
"We were never arrogant to assume that we
had an opportunity beyond one film at a time.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.940
That's something that Chris always would talk
about.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.480
He'd say, 'This is it.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.660
We're making one film.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:54.800
That's all we've got.'

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.160
Then when they came and said, 'You want to
go make another?'

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.109
It was fantastic, but we still said, 'This
is it.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.040
We will not get another opportunity."

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.580
Bale went on to offer that while a trilogy
was far from a given for Nolan's Dark Knight

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.120
saga, it was, if only indirectly, what the
director had in mind from the start.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:11.920
The actor shared:

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.660
"Chris had always said to me that if we were
fortunate to be able to make three we would

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.160
stop.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.700
'Let's walk away after that.'

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.220
Then when they [Warner Bros.] inevitably came
to us and said, 'How about a No. 4?'

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.340
I said, 'No.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.780
We have to stick to Chris' dream, which was
always to, hopefully, do a trilogy.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.440
Let's not stretch too far and become overindulgent
and go for a fourth.'"

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.880
As we all know, Bale and Nolan got two more
chapters of their Gotham-set saga after the

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.700
success of 2005's Batman Begins.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.770
With 2008's game-changing The Dark Knight
and 2011's trilogy-capping The Dark Knight

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:48.329
Rises, they also happened to fashion one of
the most celebrated superhero trilogies in

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:49.560
the history of cinema.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.640
While The Dark Knight — the first of the
series' sequels — is still widely regarded

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.780
as being among the greatest superhero movies
ever made, adoration isn't quite as strong

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.400
for Nolan's The Dark Knight Rises, which continues
to divide both critics and fans.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.459
Truth be told, part of the problem with The
Dark Knight Rises is that the film itself

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:11.560
feels a bit overstuffed, and teeters frequently
on the edge of overindulgent in its own right.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.080
Perhaps Bale and Nolan were smart to end their
franchise when they did.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.640
Still, there's little doubt that fans of Nolan
and Bale's Dark Knight trilogy would've turned

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.590
out en masse for even a massively "overindulgent"
Dark Knight 4, though such a film may have

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.680
also tarnished the trilogy's mostly impeccable
legacy.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.720
While Nolan and Bale's trilogy forever changed
the state of Batman's cinematic world — particularly

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.950
2008's The Dark Knight, which was anchored
by Heath Ledger's unforgettable turn as the

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.700
Joker …

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.020
"All you care about is money.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.080
This town deserves a better class of criminal
— and I'm gonna give it to 'em."

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.700
2021 will see moviegoers treated to a fresh,
neo-noir take on the Dark Knight coming from

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.280
Cloverfield and Dawn of the Planet of the
Apes director Matt Reeves.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.680
Simply titled The Batman, the flick will see
the beloved comic book character get back

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.360
to his detective roots as he seeks to spread
his own version of justice across the streets

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.000
of Gotham.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.099
While Reeves is still rounding out the cast
for The Batman, he's already surrounding lead

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:16.019
Robert Pattinson with a legit murderer's row
of venerated acting talent — with Paul Dano

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.470
on board to play the Riddler, Zoë Kravitz
set to appear as Catwoman, Jeffrey Wright

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:25.220
portraying Commissioner Gordon, and Andy Serkis
stepping into the role of Alfred Pennyworth.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.540
With talent like that on board, it's safe
to say that Batman is now in the best hands

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.550
since Nolan and Bale brought their trilogy
to a close.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.370
The Batman is currently slated to shoot in
2020, and will arrive in theaters on June

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.200
25, 2021.

